{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527395360,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd83e9effePxIaBWu6n6TUfR","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22afb9f002PPWSPM8ZL5u6m0","sessionID":"ses_e2dd83e9effePxIaBWu6n6TUfR","messageID":"msg_1d22ae6c5001QxwvSpALlzCcOJ","type":"tool","id":"call_252e267ed55246788337ff2c","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md, lines 1-236\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.6\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] Every outcome the request and its amendments ask for maps to a criterion that passed, or the close-out names it as not done. Reread the request to find them, because the criteria list can miss one.\n179: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n180:       output, no unrelated file.\n181: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n182:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n187: \n188: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. What it holds is listed once, in\n189: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n190: \n191: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n192: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n193: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n194: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n195: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n196: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n197: \n198: ---\n199: \n200: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n201: \n202: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n203: \n204: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n205: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n206: that looks like a different bug.\n207: \n208: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n209: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n210: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n211: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n212: defect. A turn recalled through the history lookup is a claim too: it shows what was\n213: true when it ran, not what is true now.\n214: \n215: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n216: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n217: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n222: \n223: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n224: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n225: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n226: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n227: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n228: - [ ] The close-out names any known residual risk the operator must weigh. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n229: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n230: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n231: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n232: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n233: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n234: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n235: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n236: - [ ] I reread the request, and every outcome it asks for is met or named as not done."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527394719,"end":1794527395360}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527395364,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd83e9effePxIaBWu6n6TUfR","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22afcf8001hxe8PC5FBn8hUZ","sessionID":"ses_e2dd83e9effePxIaBWu6n6TUfR","messageID":"msg_1d22ae6c5001QxwvSpALlzCcOJ","type":"tool","id":"call_43eaa063d2b04a01a5ddfe13","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md, lines 1-220\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication handoff\"\n3: description: \"End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"what do i do now\"\n6:   - \"end of turn handback\"\n7:   - \"next steps for the operator\"\n8:   - \"who does what next\"\n9:   - \"the reply ended with nothing to do\"\n10:   - \"buried the ask\"\n11:   - \"i asked in prose\"\n12:   - \"structured choice instead of prose\"\n13:   - \"offer options not paragraphs\"\n14:   - \"waiting on the user\"\n15:   - \"blocked on a decision\"\n16:   - \"needs your approval\"\n17:   - \"unanswered question at the end\"\n18:   - \"should i continue\"\n19:   - \"which option do you want\"\n20:   - \"handing control back\"\n21:   - \"the operator has to act\"\n22:   - \"nothing is blocked on you\"\n23:   - \"state restatement cadence\"\n24:   - \"where were we\"\n25:   - \"catch me up\"\n26:   - \"closing contract\"\n27:   - \"one concrete next action\"\n28:   - \"what is in flight\"\n29:   - \"still running in the background\"\n30:   - \"did it stall or is it working\"\n31:   - \"will you continue on your own\"\n32:   - \"waiting on me or on a machine\"\n33:   - \"resume condition\"\n34: importance_tier: important\n35: contextType: reference\n36: version: 1.6.0.8\n37: ---\n38: \n39: # Rule: Communication handoff\n40: \n41: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before ending a turn that leaves the operator anything to do.\n42: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n43: \n44: ## Fires when\n45: \n46: - About to end a turn, of any kind, substantive or not.\n47: - About to report that work is done, blocked, partially done, or waiting on something.\n48: - About to end a turn while something you started is still running.\n49: - About to ask the operator anything.\n50: - About to state a fork, a trade-off, or two acceptable paths.\n51: - About to continue past a decision that was never actually made, which is when this rule earns its load.\n52: \n53: ## The rule\n54: \n55: **End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.**\n56: \n57: Naming is the obligation. An action the operator has to infer from a status report is an\n58: action that does not happen.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 1. THE HANDBACK IS A SEPARATE THING FROM THE STATUS\n63: \n64: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status, a read-only turn included. `AGENTS.md` §10\n65: requires it and this section lists what it holds. Say each part briefly:\n66: \n67: 1. **What ran or was read, and what it returned**, with the receipts from\n68:    [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §1.\n69: 2. **What is inferred** rather than observed.\n70: 3. **What only the operator can verify.**\n71: 4. **The state of the work:** edited / committed / pushed / dirty, and which branch.\n72: 5. **Known residual risk.** Any risk the operator must weigh before relying on the work, such as an untested path or a state that could not be checked. Write \"none known\" when there is none.\n73: \n74: What was not done belongs in the status as well, and [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §10\n75: says how to report it. The status reports what happened. The handback reports what happens next,\n76: and it is a different document.\n77: \n78: **Restate the state when the reader must re-orient.** It fires when the direction of the\n79: work changes, when two attempts at the same fix have failed or when the work resumes after\n80: a gap. The restatement says what is done, what is open and what changed, and carries the\n81: operator's standing constraints and decisions in their own words. It replaces the previous\n82: stated state. It does not summarize the reply.\n83: \n84: The failure this prevents: a reader who returns mid task and trusts a stated state that\n85: the turns since have superseded.\n86: \n87: **Position it last**, so a reader who stops halfway through a reply has still hit it.\n88: \n89: The close of the turn is a contract with two clauses. Completed work is shown, as the\n90: changed file, the passing check or the output, after the outcome that\n91: [`communication.md`](communication.md) §4 puts first. When a command ran, name the command\n92: and its exit status or result beside the claim it supports, before your reading of it. What happens next is exactly one concrete action, in the form that lets the operator do\n93: it, or the one line that says nothing is.\n94: \n95: The failure this prevents: a close out the operator reads, agrees with and acts on\n96: nothing from, because the work was claimed instead of shown and the next steps\n97: scattered, no single concrete action among them.\n98: \n99: ---\n100: \n101: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS AN OPERATOR ACTION\n102: \n103: Only things the operator does, and only things that are actually theirs.\n104: \n105: | Is an operator action | Is not |\n106: |---|---|\n107: | A decision only they can make | A decision you already made and are narrating |\n108: | A credential, an approval, an access grant | A step you could take and chose not to |\n109: | Running something you cannot run | Work you left undone and are reframing as theirs |\n110: | Reviewing a change before it ships | A summary of what you just did |\n111: \n112: Padding the list with your own remaining work is the common failure: it trains the operator\n113: to skim the one list they need to read.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 3. NOTHING TO DO IS ALSO AN ANSWER\n118: \n119: When nothing is blocked on the operator, say that in one line and stop.\n120: \n121: The failure this prevents: an operator who reads every reply twice looking for the thing they\n122: were supposed to notice.\n123: \n124: ---\n125: \n126: ## 4. WHEN THE THING YOU NEED IS A DECISION\n127: \n128: **Ask as a structured choice, through the question tool §5 names for your runtime, when all\n129: three hold:**\n130: \n131: - The alternatives are nameable, not open-ended.\n132: - The answer changes what you do next. A question that changes nothing is a delay.\n133: - You cannot resolve it from the request, the code or a sensible default.\n134: \n135: **Otherwise put it in prose and keep going.** `AGENTS.md` §3 already refuses \"should I\n136: continue?\" for a step that is clear and in scope.\n137: \n138: Recommend one option and say why.\n139: \n140: A lettered or numbered menu typed into the reply is not a structured choice when §5 names a\n141: tool for your runtime. It is the buried question in a different shape: the operator has to\n142: type the answer back, and nothing records it as a decision.\n143: \n144: The failure this prevents: two of them. A question nobody answers because it was a sentence in\n145: a paragraph, and a menu that appears for something the operator expected you to handle.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 5. THE QUESTION SURFACE IS PER RUNTIME\n150: \n151: The posture above binds on every runtime. Resolve the surface at the runtime you are in\n152: rather than assuming one.\n153: \n154: | Runtime | Surface | How this is known |\n155: |---|---|---|\n156: | Claude Code | `AskUserQuestion`, in the main conversation only. A subagent has none | Named in this repository |\n157: | Pi | `ask_user_question` from the `@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question` extension, or `question` from `pi-plan-build` | Recorded as installed in `.pi/PLUGINS.md` |\n158: | OpenCode | `question`, built in | The tool name the decision-capture sweep reads, `sweep-opencode.mjs` |\n159: | Codex | `request_user_input`, only on a turn whose tool list names it. Plan mode has it, Default mode only behind the `default_mode_request_user_input` feature, which ships off and this repository's `.codex/config.toml` turns on, and `codex exec` never | Read from the Codex 0.160.1 binary's own instructions |\n160: | Devin | `ask_user_question`. A subagent has none | `cli-devin/references/devin-tools.md` |\n161: | DSH | `ask_user_question`, in a session with an attached client. A headless run has none | `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md` |\n162: | Cursor | None | No question tool in the Cursor tool reference |\n163: | Anything else | No native surface assumed | Use the fallback below |\n164: \n165: **A session with no such surface is not exempt.** That is a runtime the table gives no tool,\n166: or a session where the tool is missing: a subagent, a headless run, `codex exec`. It falls\n167: back to a numbered list of named options with the recommendation marked, and says in one\n168: line that no question tool was available. Where the table names a tool and the session has\n169: it, the typed list is the failure §4 names, never a fallback.\n170: \n171: Do not invent a tool name to fill a gap in that table. An invented name fails silently, the\n172: question is never asked, and the turn ends looking complete. Where the row says unverified,\n173: check first or use the fallback.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 6. WHAT IS STILL RUNNING, AND WHAT RESUMES YOU\n178: \n179: **Name what is in flight.** Anything you started that is still running and not yet\n180: reportable: a background agent, a dispatched lane, a watcher, a long suite, a build. One\n181: item is a line. More than one is a small table, the item and the state it is actually in.\n182: That table is this rule's one carve-out from [`communication.md`](communication.md) §2.\n183: The state is the payload: what it is doing now, how far in it is, what it is waiting on.\n184: \n185: **Say what resumes you.** One line, of the shape \"I will continue autonomously when …\",\n186: naming the event that unblocks you: a lane returning, a suite going green, a watcher firing.\n187: \n188: **Neither is ceremony.** A turn with nothing running writes no table and claims no resume\n189: condition. A turn that genuinely needs a decision says so and stops, and never claims it\n190: will continue by itself.\n191: \n192: The failure this prevents: a turn that goes quiet while work is still running, leaving the\n193: operator unable to tell whether anything is happening or whether it is their move.\n194: \n195: ---\n196: \n197: ## 7. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n198: \n199: - **Not licence to ask more.** The bar in §4 is narrow on purpose, and `AGENTS.md` §2\n200:   consolidates whatever survives it into a single prompt.\n201: - **Not licence to stop early.** Naming what is left is never a substitute for finishing what\n202:   is yours, and `AGENTS.md` §3 refuses partial work framed as a checkpoint.\n203: - **Not a template.** No heading is required, no fixed wording. A reader who can act is the\n204:   only test.\n205: \n206: ---\n207: \n208: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n209: \n210: - [ ] The status names what ran, what is inferred, what only the operator can verify, the state of the work and any known residual risk. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n211: - [ ] The turn ends by naming what is the operator's to do, or by saying nothing is.\n212: - [ ] Every item on that list is theirs, not mine reframed.\n213: - [ ] The handback is last, and survives a reader who stops halfway.\n214: - [ ] Anything asked as a structured choice passes all three conditions in §4.\n215: - [ ] Any choice I offered carries a recommendation and a reason.\n216: - [ ] Nothing was handed back that I could have decided or done myself.\n217: - [ ] No question-tool name appears that I did not confirm for the runtime I am in.\n218: - [ ] Where the direction changed, two fixes failed or the work resumed after a gap, I restated what is done, what is open and what changed.\n219: - [ ] Anything I started that is still running is named with the state it is in, and nothing appears there that has already finished.\n220: - [ ] A claim that I will continue on my own names the event that resumes me, and no such claim stands on a turn that needs an operator decision.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md, lines 1-220\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication handoff\"\n3: description: \"End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"what do i do now\"\n6:   - \"end of turn handback\"\n7:   - \"next steps for the operator\"\n8:   - \"who does what next\"\n9:   - \"the reply ended with nothing to do\"\n10:   - \"buried the ask\"\n11:   - \"i asked in prose\"\n12:   - \"structured choice instead of prose\"\n13:   - \"offer options not paragraphs\"\n14:   - \"waiting on the user\"\n15:   - \"blocked on a decision\"\n16:   - \"needs your approval\"\n17:   - \"unanswered question at the end\"\n18:   - \"should i continue\"\n19:   - \"which option do you want\"\n20:   - \"handing control back\"\n21:   - \"the operator has to act\"\n22:   - \"nothing is blocked on you\"\n23:   - \"state restatement cadence\"\n24:   - \"where were we\"\n25:   - \"catch me up\"\n26:   - \"closing contract\"\n27:   - \"one concrete next action\"\n28:   - \"what is in flight\"\n29:   - \"still running in the background\"\n30:   - \"did it stall or is it working\"\n31:   - \"will you continue on your own\"\n32:   - \"waiting on me or on a machine\"\n33:   - \"resume condition\"\n34: importance_tier: important\n35: contextType: reference\n36: version: 1.6.0.8\n37: ---\n38: \n39: # Rule: Communication handoff\n40: \n41: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before ending a turn that leaves the operator anything to do.\n42: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n43: \n44: ## Fires when\n45: \n46: - About to end a turn, of any kind, substantive or not.\n47: - About to report that work is done, blocked, partially done, or waiting on something.\n48: - About to end a turn while something you started is still running.\n49: - About to ask the operator anything.\n50: - About to state a fork, a trade-off, or two acceptable paths.\n51: - About to continue past a decision that was never actually made, which is when this rule earns its load.\n52: \n53: ## The rule\n54: \n55: **End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.**\n56: \n57: Naming is the obligation. An action the operator has to infer from a status report is an\n58: action that does not happen.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 1. THE HANDBACK IS A SEPARATE THING FROM THE STATUS\n63: \n64: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status, a read-only turn included. `AGENTS.md` §10\n65: requires it and this section lists what it holds. Say each part briefly:\n66: \n67: 1. **What ran or was read, and what it returned**, with the receipts from\n68:    [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §1.\n69: 2. **What is inferred** rather than observed.\n70: 3. **What only the operator can verify.**\n71: 4. **The state of the work:** edited / committed / pushed / dirty, and which branch.\n72: 5. **Known residual risk.** Any risk the operator must weigh before relying on the work, such as an untested path or a state that could not be checked. Write \"none known\" when there is none.\n73: \n74: What was not done belongs in the status as well, and [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §10\n75: says how to report it. The status reports what happened. The handback reports what happens next,\n76: and it is a different document.\n77: \n78: **Restate the state when the reader must re-orient.** It fires when the direction of the\n79: work changes, when two attempts at the same fix have failed or when the work resumes after\n80: a gap. The restatement says what is done, what is open and what changed, and carries the\n81: operator's standing constraints and decisions in their own words. It replaces the previous\n82: stated state. It does not summarize the reply.\n83: \n84: The failure this prevents: a reader who returns mid task and trusts a stated state that\n85: the turns since have superseded.\n86: \n87: **Position it last**, so a reader who stops halfway through a reply has still hit it.\n88: \n89: The close of the turn is a contract with two clauses. Completed work is shown, as the\n90: changed file, the passing check or the output, after the outcome that\n91: [`communication.md`](communication.md) §4 puts first. When a command ran, name the command\n92: and its exit status or result beside the claim it supports, before your reading of it. What happens next is exactly one concrete action, in the form that lets the operator do\n93: it, or the one line that says nothing is.\n94: \n95: The failure this prevents: a close out the operator reads, agrees with and acts on\n96: nothing from, because the work was claimed instead of shown and the next steps\n97: scattered, no single concrete action among them.\n98: \n99: ---\n100: \n101: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS AN OPERATOR ACTION\n102: \n103: Only things the operator does, and only things that are actually theirs.\n104: \n105: | Is an operator action | Is not |\n106: |---|---|\n107: | A decision only they can make | A decision you already made and are narrating |\n108: | A credential, an approval, an access grant | A step you could take and chose not to |\n109: | Running something you cannot run | Work you left undone and are reframing as theirs |\n110: | Reviewing a change before it ships | A summary of what you just did |\n111: \n112: Padding the list with your own remaining work is the common failure: it trains the operator\n113: to skim the one list they need to read.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 3. NOTHING TO DO IS ALSO AN ANSWER\n118: \n119: When nothing is blocked on the operator, say that in one line and stop.\n120: \n121: The failure this prevents: an operator who reads every reply twice looking for the thing they\n122: were supposed to notice.\n123: \n124: ---\n125: \n126: ## 4. WHEN THE THING YOU NEED IS A DECISION\n127: \n128: **Ask as a structured choice, through the question tool §5 names for your runtime, when all\n129: three hold:**\n130: \n131: - The alternatives are nameable, not open-ended.\n132: - The answer changes what you do next. A question that changes nothing is a delay.\n133: - You cannot resolve it from the request, the code or a sensible default.\n134: \n135: **Otherwise put it in prose and keep going.** `AGENTS.md` §3 already refuses \"should I\n136: continue?\" for a step that is clear and in scope.\n137: \n138: Recommend one option and say why.\n139: \n140: A lettered or numbered menu typed into the reply is not a structured choice when §5 names a\n141: tool for your runtime. It is the buried question in a different shape: the operator has to\n142: type the answer back, and nothing records it as a decision.\n143: \n144: The failure this prevents: two of them. A question nobody answers because it was a sentence in\n145: a paragraph, and a menu that appears for something the operator expected you to handle.\n146: \n147: ---\n148: \n149: ## 5. THE QUESTION SURFACE IS PER RUNTIME\n150: \n151: The posture above binds on every runtime. Resolve the surface at the runtime you are in\n152: rather than assuming one.\n153: \n154: | Runtime | Surface | How this is known |\n155: |---|---|---|\n156: | Claude Code | `AskUserQuestion`, in the main conversation only. A subagent has none | Named in this repository |\n157: | Pi | `ask_user_question` from the `@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question` extension, or `question` from `pi-plan-build` | Recorded as installed in `.pi/PLUGINS.md` |\n158: | OpenCode | `question`, built in | The tool name the decision-capture sweep reads, `sweep-opencode.mjs` |\n159: | Codex | `request_user_input`, only on a turn whose tool list names it. Plan mode has it, Default mode only behind the `default_mode_request_user_input` feature, which ships off and this repository's `.codex/config.toml` turns on, and `codex exec` never | Read from the Codex 0.160.1 binary's own instructions |\n160: | Devin | `ask_user_question`. A subagent has none | `cli-devin/references/devin-tools.md` |\n161: | DSH | `ask_user_question`, in a session with an attached client. A headless run has none | `.dsh/plugins/repo-guards/README.md` |\n162: | Cursor | None | No question tool in the Cursor tool reference |\n163: | Anything else | No native surface assumed | Use the fallback below |\n164: \n165: **A session with no such surface is not exempt.** That is a runtime the table gives no tool,\n166: or a session where the tool is missing: a subagent, a headless run, `codex exec`. It falls\n167: back to a numbered list of named options with the recommendation marked, and says in one\n168: line that no question tool was available. Where the table names a tool and the session has\n169: it, the typed list is the failure §4 names, never a fallback.\n170: \n171: Do not invent a tool name to fill a gap in that table. An invented name fails silently, the\n172: question is never asked, and the turn ends looking complete. Where the row says unverified,\n173: check first or use the fallback.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 6. WHAT IS STILL RUNNING, AND WHAT RESUMES YOU\n178: \n179: **Name what is in flight.** Anything you started that is still running and not yet\n180: reportable: a background agent, a dispatched lane, a watcher, a long suite, a build. One\n181: item is a line. More than one is a small table, the item and the state it is actually in.\n182: That table is this rule's one carve-out from [`communication.md`](communication.md) §2.\n183: The state is the payload: what it is doing now, how far in it is, what it is waiting on.\n184: \n185: **Say what resumes you.** One line, of the shape \"I will continue autonomously when …\",\n186: naming the event that unblocks you: a lane returning, a suite going green, a watcher firing.\n187: \n188: **Neither is ceremony.** A turn with nothing running writes no table and claims no resume\n189: condition. A turn that genuinely needs a decision says so and stops, and never claims it\n190: will continue by itself.\n191: \n192: The failure this prevents: a turn that goes quiet while work is still running, leaving the\n193: operator unable to tell whether anything is happening or whether it is their move.\n194: \n195: ---\n196: \n197: ## 7. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n198: \n199: - **Not licence to ask more.** The bar in §4 is narrow on purpose, and `AGENTS.md` §2\n200:   consolidates whatever survives it into a single prompt.\n201: - **Not licence to stop early.** Naming what is left is never a substitute for finishing what\n202:   is yours, and `AGENTS.md` §3 refuses partial work framed as a checkpoint.\n203: - **Not a template.** No heading is required, no fixed wording. A reader who can act is the\n204:   only test.\n205: \n206: ---\n207: \n208: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n209: \n210: - [ ] The status names what ran, what is inferred, what only the operator can verify, the state of the work and any known residual risk. It says \"none known\" when there is none.\n211: - [ ] The turn ends by naming what is the operator's to do, or by saying nothing is.\n212: - [ ] Every item on that list is theirs, not mine reframed.\n213: - [ ] The handback is last, and survives a reader who stops halfway.\n214: - [ ] Anything asked as a structured choice passes all three conditions in §4.\n215: - [ ] Any choice I offered carries a recommendation and a reason.\n216: - [ ] Nothing was handed back that I could have decided or done myself.\n217: - [ ] No question-tool name appears that I did not confirm for the runtime I am in.\n218: - [ ] Where the direction changed, two fixes failed or the work resumed after a gap, I restated what is done, what is open and what changed.\n219: - [ ] Anything I started that is still running is named with the state it is in, and nothing appears there that has already finished.\n220: - [ ] A claim that I will continue on my own names the event that resumes me, and no such claim stands on a turn that needs an operator decision."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527395064,"end":1794527395364}}}}
